AB  - We consider the problem of choosing a set of locations of a public good on the real line $$\mathbb R$$ R when agents have single-peaked preferences over points. We ordinally extend preferences over compact subsets of $$\mathbb R$$ R , and extend the results of Ching and Thomson ( 1996 ), Vohra ( 1999 ), and Klaus ( 2001 ) to choice correspondences. We show that efficiency and replacement-dominance characterize the class of target point functions (Corollary 2 ) while efficiency and population-monotonicity characterize the class of target set correspondences (Theorem 1 ).
